66 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (Dandruff, is a condition of the scalp that results in dry, itchy, fl aky skin lesions, and is associated with dysbiosis of the scalp microbiome \(1\). This dysbiosis is most characterised by an increase in Malassezia spp. and a decrease in ben- efi cial microbes such as C.acnes \(2\). Charles-Louis Malassez fi rst proposed a link between fungi overgrowth and dandruff in 1874 \(3\). The microbiome dysbiosis can also result in infl amma- tion of the scalp skin, exacerbating the itch response \(4\).)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
67 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (Current treatments for dandruff focus on actives to target the Malassezia spp., sebum and oil production in the scalp, and soothing of infl ammation and itch- iness \(5\). Therefore, when investigating new active ingredients and formulations to control dandruff, it is important to consider the impact on the microbiome balance and infl ammatory responses in the scalp.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
68 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
69 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (The scalp microbiome is a complex consortium of bacteria, fungi/yeast, arachae and viruses. The microbiome helps to regulate pH in the scalp, protect the scalp from infections and to support healthy hair growth. An imbalanced mi- crobiome on the scalp can present as itchiness, fl akiness, redness or even conditions such as dandruff, seborrheic )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
70 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (dermatitis and even hair loss.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
71 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (In this article, we use an in vitro human skin model \(Labskin-S\) colonised with a scalp microbiome \(C.acnes, S.epi- dermidis, C.striatum and Malassezia spp.\) to investigate how ingredients used in hair care products can alter the microbiome composition and the effects on pro-infl ammatory markers released in the skin.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
72 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (We showed that when targeting key microbes in the scalp microbiome, for example anti-dandruff actives against Malassezia spp., it is important to consider, not only the effects on the microbe, but also on the scalp skin.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
73 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
74 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (The aim of this study was to use a 3D in vitro human skin model populated with the main microbes found on the human scalp to investigate the im- pact of two novel test items; one de- signed to target Malassezia spp. and the other to maintain the balance of microbes.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
